# Design System Rules

Before styling a screen, inspect global.css, the installed NativeWind version, theme configuration, typography, and shared UI components.

## Tokens
- Reuse existing colors, typography, spacing, radii, borders, and theme tokens.
- Preserve the current light and dark theme behavior.
- Do not invent colors or spacing values when an appropriate token already exists.
- Do not modify global.css to solve an isolated component problem.
- Add a reusable token only when the design system genuinely needs it.

## Shared Components
- Search for existing Screen, Text, Button, Input, Card, Header, EmptyState, Loading, and other shared components.
- Extend existing components when the new behavior is reusable.
- Keep feature-specific components local until reuse is justified.
- Do not create a parallel component library without a clear reason.

## Typography
- Follow the project's font and typography scale.
- Establish hierarchy using size, weight, spacing, and contrast.
- Keep supporting text visually subordinate.
- Ensure Arabic, English, and mixed-language text remain legible.

## Composition
- Do not wrap every section in a Card.
- Use grouped rows, separators, whitespace, lists, and surfaces intentionally.
- Keep alignment and horizontal margins consistent.
- Use restrained decoration and a clear primary action.

## NativeWind
- Follow conventions supported by the installed version.
- Preserve the existing global.css and Tailwind integration.
- Avoid web-only CSS assumptions in native components.
- Keep conditional class names compatible with the project's build setup.

If a token or component is missing, identify the gap and propose the smallest reusable change that fits the existing design system.